@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia - the Spiritism of Allan Kardec
#
# Order of the file: the tradition, then the operation and the capacity it is
# supposed to yield, then the control the founder puts in place, then the two
# things he rules out and what their combination does to the whole. The
# instance was registered in traditions/registry-diaspora.ttl and is moved
# here now that passages have been read.
#
# The registration said this transmission submits itself to examination in its
# own founding text and is therefore the natural first object of the project's
# examination layer. That is right, and the reading found the reverse of it in
# the same works: the founder states a rule of verification and, in another
# chapter, forbids the one question that would apply it.
#

pmt:Spiritism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Spiritism"@en, "Spiritismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission founded by Kardec on answers obtained through mediums and arranged as a doctrine, with a stated rule for admitting a principle into that doctrine and a stated rule for judging which spirit is speaking."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Le Livre des Médiums, chapter XXIV, sections 263 to 268, and chapter XXVI, sections 286 to 288; L'Évangile selon le spiritisme, the introduction, part II, under the heading of the universal control of the teaching of the spirits; La Genèse, chapter I, articles 51 to 55. Not opened: Le Livre des Esprits, the chapter on obsession in Le Livre des Médiums, and the Revue Spirite. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#497."@en ;
    skos:note "The editions are a difficulty this file does not hide. Kardec renumbered and enlarged Le Livre des Esprits between 1857 and 1860, from about five hundred questions to about a thousand, and everything since is cited by the later numbering. The digital witnesses read here carry the standard numbering and do not name the printing they were set from. The sections cited are therefore findable but the edition is not stated, which SPEC section 10 requires. See prima-materia#AUSGABE."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route stays at documented, as the registration had it. Nothing in this transmission is a candidate for independent attestation: it is a nineteenth-century European construction with named authors, a dated founding text and a documented reception, and its value to this graph lies elsewhere - in being the one body of material in the collection that states its own rule of verification in writing."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kardec, Le Livre des Esprits; Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums; Kardec, L'Évangile selon le spiritisme; Kardec, La Genèse; the Revue Spirite, 1858-1869" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 263-268 (French text in the standard numbering; the digital witness read does not name its printing)" .

#
# The operation and the capacity it is supposed to yield
#

pmp:Evocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Evocation"@en, "Evokation"@de ;
    skos:definition "The putting of questions to a spirit through a medium. The founder makes the form of the question part of the operation: it is to be clear, precise and put in a logical order, and the answer given depends on how it was asked."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "évocation"@fr ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, sections 286-288" .

pmc:DistinctionDesEsprits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Telling a good spirit from a bad one"@en, "Die Unterscheidung guter und schlechter Geister"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the whole system rests on, since every claim in it arrives through a communication that may be false. The criterion given is not evidential but linguistic and moral: spirits are judged as men are judged, by their language, and goodness and benevolence are held to be the essential attributes of purified spirits."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "distinction des bons et des mauvais Esprits"@fr ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 263-264" .

pmp:CritiqueRigoureuse a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Rigorous critique of a communication"@en, "Die strenge Kritik einer Mitteilung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation the founder offers as the means of that distinction, and he claims for it both exclusivity and certainty in one sentence: this means is the only one, but it is infallible, because there is no bad communication that can withstand a rigorous critique. The marks it works with are given as a numbered list, among them that good spirits never command but advise; that they do not flatter, while bad ones praise extravagantly and stimulate vanity while preaching humility; that an elevated spirit does not contradict itself across time and place; and that good spirits say only what they know and are silent or confess their ignorance, while bad ones speak of everything with a false assurance."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim of infallibility is the strongest single sentence this transmission makes and the one most open to examination, because it asserts that no false communication can survive the procedure. It is entered in the graph as it stands, without softening, since the value of holding it is that it can be held against cases."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 266-267" .

#
# The control the founder puts in place
#

pmc:ControleUniversel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The universal control of the teaching of the spirits"@en, "Die allgemeine Kontrolle der Geisterlehre"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rule of verification the transmission sets for itself: concordance in the teaching of the spirits is the best control. It is stated with its conditions and with its limits. The revelations must come spontaneously, through a large number of mediums unknown to one another, and from different places. A single medium questioning many spirits is expressly not a sufficient guarantee, because one obsessing spirit could present the same content under different names; and mediums of a single centre are not sufficient either, because they may stand under a common influence. The criterion is restricted to what attaches to the principles of the doctrine and does not extend to communications on secondary matters."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "contrôle universel de l'enseignement des Esprits"@fr ;
    skos:note "Both exclusions are the founder's own and both are exactly the ones an outside critic would raise. That is worth marking, because it makes the rule better than the practice that followed it: the same author describes elsewhere how the scattered material was gathered and weighed at a single centre, and that centre was himself."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, L'Évangile selon le spiritisme, introduction, section II, sub-heading Contrôle universel de l'enseignement des Esprits" .

pmc:PrincipeDeLaDoctrine a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A principle's standing as part of the doctrine"@en, "Die Zugehörigkeit eines Grundsatzes zur Lehre"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the control decides. The transmission holds that the teaching had to be collective and not individual, that the spirits divided the work by scattering the subjects of study across many places, and that a centre formed of itself to compare what came in and to distinguish an individual opinion from the general teaching."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, La Genèse, les miracles et les prédictions selon le spiritisme, chapitre I, articles 51-54" .

pmc:SpiritismPrinciplePresupposesControl a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "A principle enters the doctrine only after the control of generality"@en, "Ein Grundsatz gehört zur Lehre erst nach der allgemeinen Kontrolle"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:PrincipeDeLaDoctrine ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ControleUniversel ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The consequence is stated as a disqualification and is quotable: any principle that has not received the consecration of the control of generality cannot be considered an integral part of the doctrine. The same article adds the other half, that if new discoveries showed spiritism to be in error on a point, it would modify itself on that point." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism ;
    skos:note "This is the passage on which the registration's judgement rested, and it holds: a transmission that writes down the condition under which it would change is doing something almost nothing else in this collection does. What the next section of this file records is what the same author did with that opening elsewhere."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, La Genèse, les miracles et les prédictions selon le spiritisme, chapitre I, article 55" .

#
# The two things the founder rules out
#

pmc:SigneMaterielCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "No material sign proves who is speaking"@en, "Kein materielles Zeichen beweist, wer spricht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission's own warning against the evidential test. Superior spirits, it says, have no signs by which to be recognised other than the superiority of their ideas and their language, and every spirit can imitate a material sign. It adds that spirits take the names of saints and of famous persons, and that this is not fraud where the intention is good, so that the name is no proof either."@en ;
    skos:note "Two of the three things an outside investigator would reach for - a signature or handwriting, and a name - are ruled out by the transmission itself before anyone else can propose them. What is left is the third, the quality of the language, which is the one an outsider cannot measure."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Evocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, section 268, questions 3-6 and 23" .

pmc:QuestionDEpreuve a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The test question"@en, "Die Prüfungsfrage"@de ;
    skos:definition "A question put in order to try the perspicacity of the spirits. The transmission classes it with the useless questions and with those put out of curiosity, and states the outcome: to those the spirits do not reply, and they withdraw. The founder warns against them expressly when he sets out how a question is to be framed."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "question d'épreuve"@fr ;
    skos:note "The French word épreuve carries both senses in this literature and the transmission uses both: the trial a soul undergoes in an incarnation, which it affirms, and the test question, which it forbids. A translation that renders them alike loses the distinction at the point where it matters most."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, sections 286 and 288, item 3" .

pmc:EpreuveCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Against putting the spirits to the test"@en, "Gegen die Prüfung der Geister"@de ;
    skos:definition "The instruction that follows from it. Questions asked from a motive of curiosity or of test are not to be put; the spirits addressed do not answer them and go away, and the transmission adds that such questions please the imperfect spirits, who are amused by them."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule is self-sealing in a way the graph should record plainly rather than argue about. A superior spirit that withdraws when tested and an absent spirit produce the same observation; and a spirit that does answer a test question is by that answer classed as imperfect. Both outcomes are accounted for in advance, so the test yields no information either way."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Evocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, section 288, item 3" .

#
# What the combination does to the whole
#

pmc:SpiritismControlExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "The control that cannot be applied"@en, "Die Kontrolle, die sich nicht anwenden lässt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the transmission's own rule of verification. Two passages by the same author, in two works, stand against each other in the way that matters here. One states a control and states it well: concordance of spontaneous teaching, from many mediums unknown to one another, in different places, restricted to fundamental principles, with the express refusal to count a single medium or a single centre. The other forbids the question put in order to test, and settles both outcomes in advance - the spirits addressed do not answer and withdraw, and a spirit that does answer such a question is thereby classed as imperfect."@en ;
    skos:note "The two rules do not contradict each other on their face and the finding is not that they do. The finding is what they leave: a criterion that can be satisfied by agreement and cannot be strained by a question. Agreement among mediums who share a doctrine, a vocabulary and an expectation is the outcome to be explained, not the evidence that explains it, and the one operation that would tell a real concordance from a shared expectation is the one the transmission rules out."@en ;
    skos:note "The state on this protocol should be pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate and is not, and the reason is a defect in the shapes rather than a judgement about the claim. pm:CasesRequireCounterSearchShape groups that state with the two states that report cases and therefore demands a carried counter-search; but the verdict that a claim excludes no outcome is a verdict about the form of the claim and needs no evidence, so there is nothing for a counter-search to have searched. Setting a counter-search state here would assert a search that did not happen. The protocol therefore stands at procedure without cases, which understates it, and the defect is entered at prima-materia#SHAPE rather than worked around."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:ControleUniversel ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A passage in this transmission in which a concordance of independent mediums is reported and then set aside as insufficient on some ground other than the moral quality of the communication; or a recorded case in which a test question was put, answered, and the answer accepted as evidence rather than as a mark of an imperfect spirit. Either would show that the control can be strained and that the exclusion of the test question is not doing the work described here." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol formed from two passages of the same author read at the wording; no case series has been collected and no external examination has been read" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, L'Évangile selon le spiritisme, introduction, section II, sub-heading Contrôle universel de l'enseignement des Esprits", "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, section 288, item 3" .

pmc:EvocationAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "The operation places the agency with the spirit"@en, "Die Operation verortet die Handlungsträgerschaft beim Geist"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:Evocation ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situationGround "The medium supplies the hand or the voice and the content is the spirit's. The transmission makes the point itself at the place where it matters: the spirits decide whether to answer, they withdraw from a question they dislike, and they give proof only when it suits them. What the sitter controls is the form of the question, not the reply." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference .
